CAT = "auforkliftLoads"; SEG = "LDS"
Q = [
("What is the load centre of a forklift load?",
 "Fork face to the load's centre of gravity", ["Front axle centre to the load's front edge", "Mast to the back of the counterweight", "Fork tip to the heel of the fork"],
 "The load centre is measured horizontally from the vertical face of the forks to the load's centre of gravity. The data plate rates capacity at a stated load centre."),
("At what conditions does a data plate state the truck's rated capacity?",
 "A set load centre and height", ["Any load centre or height", "The truck's top travel speed", "Loads carried out on the fork tips"],
 "Rated capacity applies only at the load centre and lift height shown. Change either and the safe capacity changes."),
("What happens to a forklift's safe capacity as the load centre moves further from the mast?",
 "It decreases", ["It increases", "It stays the same", "It doubles"],
 "A longer load centre gives the load more leverage about the front wheels, so the same weight tips the truck sooner. Capacity goes down."),
("On many data plates, how does capacity at maximum lift height compare with capacity lower down?",
 "It is lower", ["It is higher", "It is the same", "It is not shown"],
 "Lifting higher raises the combined centre of gravity and reduces stability, so many plates and load charts reduce capacity above a set height."),
("A counterbalance forklift's stability triangle is formed by which points?",
 "Front wheels and the rear axle pivot", ["All four wheels of the truck", "Rear wheels and the mast base", "Front wheels and the rear counterweight"],
 "The steer axle pivots at its centre, so the truck is supported at three points: the two front wheels and the rear axle pivot. The combined centre of gravity must stay inside that triangle."),
("What happens if the combined centre of gravity moves outside the stability triangle?",
 "The truck can tip over", ["The forks drop slowly to the floor", "The engine stalls", "The steering locks"],
 "Once the combined centre of gravity of truck and load leaves the stability triangle, the truck tips. Overloading, raised loads, turns and slopes can all move it out."),
("What is the counterweight on a counterbalance forklift for?",
 "Balancing the load on the forks", ["Adding traction for speed", "Holding the battery in place", "Shielding the operator from impacts"],
 "The counterweight sits behind the rear axle to balance the load in front of the front wheels. It is built for the rated capacity only."),
("How does fitting an attachment usually affect the truck's safe capacity?",
 "It usually reduces it", ["It always increases it", "It has no effect on it", "It is only lowered on ramps"],
 "An attachment adds weight in front of the front wheels and often moves the load centre forward, so net capacity drops. Use the capacity on the plate for that combination."),
("A pallet holds 24 cartons of 30 kg each. The pallet weighs 20 kg. What is the total load?",
 "740 kg", ["720 kg", "760 kg", "480 kg"],
 "24 x 30 kg = 720 kg of cartons, plus the 20 kg pallet makes 740 kg. Leaving out the pallet gives 720 kg."),
("A pallet has 6 layers of 8 cartons, each carton 25 kg, on a 30 kg pallet. What is the total load?",
 "1230 kg", ["1200 kg", "380 kg", "1260 kg"],
 "6 x 8 = 48 cartons, and 48 x 25 kg = 1200 kg, plus the 30 kg pallet makes 1230 kg. Adding the layers and cartons instead of multiplying gives 380 kg."),
("You weigh one carton at 18.5 kg. The pallet holds 40 cartons and weighs 25 kg. What is the total load?",
 "765 kg", ["740 kg", "790 kg", "715 kg"],
 "40 x 18.5 kg = 740 kg, plus the 25 kg pallet makes 765 kg. Weighing one carton and multiplying is an accepted way to estimate load weight."),
("Four drums of 210 kg each sit on a 20 kg pallet. What is the total load?",
 "860 kg", ["840 kg", "880 kg", "234 kg"],
 "4 x 210 kg = 840 kg, plus the 20 kg pallet makes 860 kg."),
("Which of these is a reliable source for a load's weight?",
 "Its consignment note", ["Its pallet footprint", "Its outer dimensions", "Its packaging colour"],
 "Load weight can come from labels and markings on the load, consignment notes, running sheets, weighbridge dockets or weighing a carton and calculating. Size alone does not show weight."),
("A load has no label, markings or paperwork showing its weight. What should you do?",
 "Find its weight before lifting", ["Lift it and see if the truck copes", "Judge it by its size", "Tilt back hard and try it"],
 "Never lift a load of unknown weight. Weigh it, weigh a unit and calculate, or get the weight from the sender before lifting."),
("The load chart shows 2500 kg at a 500 mm load centre and 2000 kg at 600 mm. A 2200 kg load has its centre at 600 mm. Can you lift it?",
 "No, it's over the 600 mm limit", ["Yes, it's under 2500 kg", "Yes, if the mast is tilted fully back", "Yes, if you drive slowly"],
 "At a 600 mm load centre the limit is 2000 kg, and 2200 kg is over it. The 2500 kg figure only applies at 500 mm."),
("The chart shows 3000 kg up to 3300 mm lift and 2600 kg from 3300 to 4500 mm, both at 500 mm. A 2800 kg load at 500 mm must go to 4000 mm. Can you do it?",
 "No, the limit there is 2600 kg", ["Yes, the limit is 3000 kg", "Yes, if lifted very slowly", "Yes, with the mast tilted right back"],
 "At 4000 mm the chart gives 2600 kg, so 2800 kg is over capacity. The 3000 kg rating only applies up to 3300 mm."),
("The chart shows 1800 kg at a 500 mm load centre and 1500 kg at 600 mm. A 1400 kg load has its centre at 600 mm. Can you lift it?",
 "Yes, it is under 1500 kg", ["No, it must be at 500 mm", "No, it is over the chart", "Only with an attachment fitted"],
 "At 600 mm the limit is 1500 kg and the load is 1400 kg, so it is within capacity as long as the height and other conditions on the chart are met."),
("A load 1200 mm deep, evenly spread, sits fully back on the forks. What is its load centre?",
 "600 mm", ["1200 mm", "500 mm", "300 mm"],
 "For an evenly spread load against the fork face, the centre of gravity is halfway along its depth: 1200 mm divided by 2 is 600 mm."),
("The standard Australian pallet measures about:",
 "1165 x 1165 mm", ["1200 x 800 mm", "1219 x 1016 mm", "1000 x 1000 mm"],
 "The Australian standard pallet is about 1165 mm square. 1200 x 800 mm is the European pallet and 1219 x 1016 mm is the North American 48 by 40 inch pallet."),
("An evenly loaded standard Australian pallet sits fully back on the forks. Roughly where is its load centre?",
 "About 583 mm", ["About 500 mm", "About 1165 mm", "About 400 mm"],
 "Half of 1165 mm is about 583 mm. That is beyond a 500 mm rated load centre, so check the chart for the longer centre before lifting a heavy pallet."),
("A load's heavy end is placed away from the mast. What does this do?",
 "Increases the load centre", ["Decreases the load centre", "Makes no real difference", "Raises the rated capacity"],
 "Moving the weight away from the fork face moves the centre of gravity out, which lengthens the load centre and cuts the safe capacity."),
("Why handle a part-filled liquid container with extra care?",
 "The liquid surges and shifts", ["Liquid is always overweight", "It always shortens the load centre", "It cools the hydraulic oil"],
 "Liquid is a dynamic load: it surges when you brake, accelerate or turn, moving the centre of gravity. Drive smoothly and slowly."),
("Before lifting an odd-shaped machine part, what must you work out?",
 "Where its centre of gravity is", ["Only its overall length", "Only its overall height", "Only the type of packaging it has"],
 "An irregular load's centre of gravity may not be in the middle. Find it so you can position the forks and judge the load centre."),
("Why tilt the mast back slightly after lifting a load?",
 "To cradle the load on the backrest", ["To raise the rated capacity", "To lengthen the load centre", "To lift it a little higher off the floor"],
 "A slight back tilt settles the load against the load backrest and keeps it from sliding forward when braking. It does not change the rated capacity."),
("What is the risk of tilting the mast forward with a load raised high?",
 "The truck can tip forward", ["The forks can lock up", "The battery may disconnect", "The load gets lighter"],
 "Forward tilt moves the load and the combined centre of gravity forward, and at height that can carry it past the front wheels. Only tilt forward with the load over the stack or rack."),
("Why does turning with a raised load risk a sideways tip?",
 "A high centre of gravity shifts out", ["The steering gets much heavier to turn", "The engine loses power", "The load gets lighter"],
 "Raising a load lifts the combined centre of gravity, and turning forces push it sideways. A high centre of gravity leaves the stability triangle more easily."),
("Why can an empty counterbalance forklift tip sideways in a sharp, fast turn?",
 "Its weight sits near the rear", ["Its forks are too light", "Its mast is too tall and heavy", "Its tyres are too wide"],
 "Unloaded, the centre of gravity sits near the rear axle pivot, where the stability triangle is narrowest. A fast turn can push it outside the triangle."),
("During a trial lift the rear wheels start to lift off the ground. What should you do?",
 "Lower the load at once", ["Have someone sit on the back", "Lift faster to clear it", "Tilt the mast forward"],
 "Rear wheels lifting means the load is over capacity for that load centre. Lower it carefully and reassess; never add people or weight as counterweight."),
("Why do a trial lift before moving a load?",
 "To check it is stable", ["To warm up the engine oil", "To test the horn and beeper", "To set the travel speed limit"],
 "A trial lift raises the load just clear of the surface so you can check that the truck and load are stable and the load is safe to move."),
("Can you add extra counterweight to lift a heavier load?",
 "No, never modify the truck", ["Yes, with sandbags", "Yes, with a person on it", "Yes, up to 10 percent extra weight"],
 "Adding counterweight overloads the axle, tyres, mast and brakes the truck was designed for. Only the manufacturer can approve changes to capacity."),
("How do fork extensions (slippers) used for a long load usually affect safe capacity?",
 "They reduce it", ["They increase it", "They have no effect", "They double it"],
 "Extensions let the load sit further from the fork face, increasing the load centre, so capacity drops. They must be rated and suited to the forks."),
("A dock plate is rated 4000 kg. The truck weighs 3600 kg and the load 900 kg. Can you cross it?",
 "No, 4500 kg exceeds it", ["Yes, the load is under 4000 kg", "Yes, if you cross slowly", "Yes, only the load counts"],
 "The plate carries the truck and load together: 3600 kg + 900 kg = 4500 kg, which is over its 4000 kg rating."),
("Your truck can lift 2000 kg. Bags weigh 45 kg each and the pallet weighs 25 kg. What is the most bags you can load?",
 "43", ["44", "45", "40"],
 "2000 kg - 25 kg = 1975 kg, and 1975 / 45 = 43.9, so 43 bags (1960 kg in total). 44 bags would make 2005 kg, over the limit."),
("A pack of 400 bricks at 3 kg each sits on a 20 kg pallet. What is the total load?",
 "1220 kg", ["1200 kg", "1180 kg", "423 kg"],
 "400 x 3 kg = 1200 kg, plus the 20 kg pallet makes 1220 kg."),
("The truck's plate for an attachment shows 1800 kg. The attachment's own plate says 1500 kg. What is the working limit?",
 "1500 kg, the lower value", ["1800 kg, the truck value", "3300 kg, the sum", "1650 kg, the average"],
 "Always work to the lower of the truck-with-attachment capacity and the attachment's own rating. Here that is 1500 kg."),
("The load chart lists capacities at 500 mm and 600 mm. Your load centre is 550 mm. Which figure do you use?",
 "The 600 mm figure", ["The 500 mm figure", "The average of both", "Whichever is higher"],
 "When the load centre falls between columns, use the next longer load centre, which gives the lower, safer capacity."),
("How far apart should the forks be set?",
 "As wide as the load allows", ["As close as possible", "Always 300 mm apart, centred", "Touching in the middle"],
 "Spreading the forks as wide as the load allows supports it evenly and keeps it stable from side to side."),
("How far should the forks go under a pallet?",
 "Fully, load against the backrest", ["Halfway in, to make turning easier", "Just the fork tips", "Until the pallet tips back"],
 "Inserting the forks fully, with the load against the backrest, keeps the load centre short and the load secure."),
("Why can braking hard with a raised load tip the truck forward?",
 "Momentum shifts the load forward", ["Brakes lock the rear wheels", "The mast tilts back", "The counterweight gets heavier at speed"],
 "When the truck stops suddenly, the load keeps moving and throws the combined centre of gravity forward, which at height can tip the truck."),
("The top cartons on a pallet are leaning and loose. What should you do?",
 "Restack or secure them first", ["Lift slowly and keep going", "Tilt forward to balance them", "Drive faster to save time"],
 "An unstable load can fall from the forks onto you or others. Restack, wrap or strap it before lifting."),
]
